@@ -0,0 +1,1413 @@
+//===- DeduBBCallReturn.cpp - DeduBB ranges and Call-Return folds ---------===//
+//
+// Part of the LLVM Project, under the Apache License v2.0 with LLVM Exceptions.
+// See https://llvm.org/LICENSE.txt for license information.
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
+//
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
+//
+// This pass applies the DeduBB Call-Return directives (bbmcr/bbfcr, see
+// DeduBBDirectives.h). A group of byte-identical blocks shares one copy of
+// their body, everything before their branches, as a routine that returns.
+// Every block of the group, the master's own included, keeps its branches and
+// calls that routine instead of running its body:
+//
+//   block:  <body>               block:  callq DeduBB.master.cr.K
+//           <branches>    =>             <branches>
+//
+//   DeduBB.cr.masters:  ...
+//   DeduBB.master.cr.K: <body>
+//                       retq
+//
+// A body that makes calls is entered with 16 bytes pushed instead of 8, so
+// that its calls see the stack alignment they saw in the block:
+//
+//           pushq %rax                  (8 bytes of padding; %rax is only read)
+//           callq DeduBB.master.cr.K
+//           leaq  8(%rsp), %rsp         (drops the padding; EFLAGS untouched)
+//
+// The master is emitted from the bytes Step 1 compared, which the directive
+// carries, with its direct calls made again by name: it computes exactly what
+// every block of the group computed in the binary the directives were made
+// from. It does not depend on how the master's own block was compiled, which
+// may differ from its bytes: the linker rewrites some code, such as accesses
+// to thread-local variables.
+//
+// A block calls the master only if its own instructions are known to produce
+// those same bytes: they reference no symbol other than the targets of their
+// direct calls (anything else would be filled in by the linker, differently
+// in another place), they are as many as the directive says, and they call the
+// same functions. Then:
+//  * The body runs unchanged in the master. Step 1 made sure it does not read
+//    or write %rsp, %rip or the shadow-stack pointer other than through its
+//    calls, so it does not care where it runs or that %rsp is 8 or 16 bytes
+//    lower: its memory accesses go through other registers, and each call
+//    reaches the same function.
+//  * Registers and flags flow into the master and back unchanged: `call`,
+//    `ret`, `push` and `lea` write neither EFLAGS nor any register but %rsp,
+//    and the stub leaves %rsp as it found it.
+//  * The stub writes only below %rsp, which is free unless the function keeps
+//    data in the red zone; such functions are left alone.
+//  * The calls inside the master see the stack aligned as before. They must
+//    not take arguments on the stack, which are now 16 bytes further away, so
+//    functions that pass stack arguments are left alone.
+//  * Unwinding through a master uses the unwind info of the function holding
+//    the masters (CFA = %rsp + 8 throughout, since a body never moves %rsp),
+//    and then the block's own function at the stub's return address, where
+//    the stub's CFI accounts for the padding if the CFA is computed from %rsp.
+//    A block with an invoke has EH labels and is never replaced, so every call
+//    that reaches a master had no landing pad before either.
+//
+// The masters live in a function of their own per module (per set of target
+// attributes), `DeduBB.cr.masters`, not in an existing function, so that they
+// get unwind info of their own. The function is in a `.text.dedubb.` section,
+// which `ld.lld -z keep-text-section-prefix` keeps apart from `.text`. The
+// master's module emits it whether or not the master's own block can call it,
+// since blocks in other modules do.
+//
+// A directive with `at=` folds a range of a block rather than its body: the
+// instructions `at` to `at + insts`, counted among those that emit code, are
+// replaced by the stub, and the rest of the block stays in place. The same
+// checks apply to the range, plus these, for the index to name the
+// instructions Step 1 decoded:
+//  * Every instruction of the block emits exactly one machine instruction
+//    (TargetInstrInfo::isDeduBBSingleInstruction): no bundle, no inline asm,
+//    and no pseudo that is lowered into code of its own, such as a
+//    thread-local access or a check. The block holds as many as the
+//    directive's `block_insts=`.
+//  * The range holds no CFI, label or frame setup or teardown instruction, and
+//    starts after the block's last frame setup instruction: from there on the
+//    stack is the body's, as in any other block, so the entry block is fine
+//    past its prologue.
+// A block may have several ranges; they are folded last first, so that each
+// keeps the instructions it names.
+//
+// The pass also applies the other kinds of range (`at=` on the directives of
+// DeduBBDirectives.h), so that all the ranges of a block are checked against
+// it as Step 1 saw it, before any is folded:
+//
+//  * Save-and-Jump runs (bbmsj/bbfsj). Like a Call-Return run, but the stub
+//    jumps to the master and the master jumps back, through the link register
+//    (R11 on x86-64):
+//
+//      block:  <before>                          DeduBB.sj.masters:  ...
+//              leaq Tail(%rip), %r11             DeduBB.master.sj.K: <run>
+//              jmp  DeduBB.master.sj.K                               jmpq *%r11
+//      Tail:   <after>
+//
+//    The block is split after the run, and its successors move to the second
+//    half. Nothing is pushed, so the master runs in the block's own frame: it
+//    may read and write the block's stack slots, though not move %rsp, call
+//    (a call clobbers the link register) or touch the link register, which
+//    must be free where the run starts. The masters are emitted from Step 1's
+//    bytes in a function without unwind info: no rule of its own describes
+//    the frame a master runs in, and without calls nothing unwinds through it.
+//    With indirect branch tracking, the master's `jmp *%r11` would need an
+//    `endbr` at its target, so the folds are left alone there.
+//
+//  * Tail-call endings (bbm/bbf). A block that ends in a return or a tail
+//    call keeps its first instructions and replaces the last ones with a jump
+//    to a label in a master block that ends the same way:
+//
+//      fold:   <before>                    master:  <its own>
+//              jmp DeduBB.master.K         DeduBB.master.K:  <the ending>
+//                                                            ret
+//
+//    The jump keeps the fold's frame, so the ending runs as if it were the
+//    fold's own: it may restore registers and pop the frame. It holds no call
+//    but a final tail call: unwinding through a call would use the master
+//    function's rules for the fold's frame, which describe it only if both
+//    functions address it alike and both have unwind tables. The label is
+//    found by counting instructions back from the end of the block, so the
+//    ending's instructions must each be emitted as one, and must reference no
+//    symbol but the tail call's target, which the directive lists, and
+//    link-time constants such as thread-local offsets
+//    (TargetInstrInfo::isDeduBBLinkTimeConstant). A master that does not check
+//    out is an error: blocks in other modules jump to it.
+//
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
+
